## Failure groups

| Group | Meaning | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| **Cross-spec cluster** | The same error in 2 or more spec files. One shared support command or selector can fix many specs at once. | Highest |
| **Broken** | The same test fails with the same error every night on record. It is not flaky, it is unaddressed. | High |
| **New regression** | It passed the night before and fails now. The skill lists the commits between the two runs as a lead. | High |
| **Intermittent** | Fails some nights and passes on others. Often not worth a code change. | Low |
| **Resolved** | Failed the night before and passes now. This proves a past fix worked. | Shown as a win |

### Words you will see

| Word | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Cascade** | A test that fails only because an earlier test in the same spec failed. Our specs use `testIsolation: false`, so each test starts on the page the previous test left. Fix the first failure and the cascades clear by themselves. |
| **Intercept** | Cypress watching an API call so the test can wait for it (`cy.intercept` and then `cy.wait("@alias")`). |
| **Not reproduced locally** | The test passes on your machine. Usually the nightly environment was slow. No edit is made. |
| **Backend / environment** | The server was slow or a job never finished. Test code cannot fix this. It is reported, not hidden. |
| **Product change** | The app changed (a renamed button, a new column) and the test did not follow. |

